-- title:
--   Finitely presented closed subschemes of affine schemes, functorially
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring (in the fixed universe $u$), let $Z$ be a scheme, and let $\iota : Z \to \operatorname{Spec} S$ be a morphism which is a closed immersion and is locally of finite presentation. The assertion is that there exists an ideal $J \subseteq S$ which is finitely generated and which represents the factorisation problem for $\iota$ on affine test objects: for every commutative ring $R$ in the same universe and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : S \to R$, there exists a morphism of schemes $y : \operatorname{Spec} R \to Z$ with $y$ followed by $\iota$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec} \varphi$ if and only if the ideal $J \cdot R$ generated by the image $\varphi(J)$ in $R$ is the zero ideal. Thus the closed subscheme cut out by $\iota$ is, as a functor on affine schemes, the vanishing locus of a finitely generated ideal of $S$, and the ideal may be chosen finitely generated precisely because $\iota$ is locally of finite presentation.
--
--   This is the functor-of-points description of a closed subscheme of an affine scheme, in the finitely generated form obtained when the closed immersion is locally of finite presentation (EGA I 4.2, EGA IV 1.4). It serves as a representability tool elsewhere in the development: it is used for schemes of coincidences of finite flat and of proper flat morphisms, and for the closed locus in a framed polarised abelian scheme where a section becomes trivial.
-- source:
